<h1>Commands Reference</h1>
<p class="subtitle">The implemented command surface, with defaults and side effects called out.</p>

<h2 id="bare">Bare <code>gate</code></h2>
<p>With no arguments, Gate does not scan the repository. If its pre-commit marker is absent, it attempts to install a pre-commit hook and prints a banner. Otherwise it shows status.</p>

<h2 id="scan"><code>gate scan [targets]</code></h2>
<div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate scan</span><span class="dim">                  # staged files</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate scan --all</span><span class="dim">            # files from git ls-files</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate scan --changed</span><span class="dim">        # upstream...HEAD, or all on comparison failure</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate scan src/app.js config/</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate scan --history 50</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate scan --format json</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate scan --format sarif</span></div>
<p>Useful flags: <code>--verify</code>, <code>--no-verify</code>, <code>--interactive</code>, <code>--entropy-threshold N</code>, <code>--format text|json|sarif</code>, and <code>--no-color</code>. <code>--staged</code> names the default behavior when no target is supplied.</p>
<p>The help text advertises <code>--max-file-size</code>, but the scan handler currently does not pass that flag into the scanner. Set <code>max_file_size</code> in <code>.gaterc</code>.</p>

<h2 id="verify"><code>gate verify [targets]</code></h2>
<p>Equivalent to the corresponding scan with <code>--verify</code>. Default scope is staged files; <code>--all</code> and explicit targets are supported. It can transmit credential values and lower inactive findings to low severity. See <a href="/gate/wiki/verification/">Credential Verification</a>.</p>

<h2 id="fix"><code>gate fix [targets]</code></h2>
<div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate fix</span><span class="dim">                   # all tracked files</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate fix --staged</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate fix --dry-run</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate fix --interactive</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate fix --undo</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate fix --file src/app.js</span></div>
<p>The default is all tracked files, whether or not <code>--all</code> is written. Explicit files/directories and <code>--entropy-threshold</code> are accepted. Fixing writes source and environment-related files; undo uses the newest local batch snapshot. Read <a href="/gate/wiki/auto-fix/">the known correctness gaps</a> first.</p>

<h2 id="incident"><code>gate incident [targets]</code></h2>
<p>Scans staged files by default, all tracked files with <code>--all</code>, or explicit targets. It generates guidance and artifacts only for findings labelled pushed. It does not execute provider or Git cleanup actions.</p>
<div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate incident --all</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate incident report gate-inc-YYYYMMDD-001</span></div>

<h2 id="init"><code>gate init</code></h2>
<p>Requires a <code>.git</code> entry in the current directory. It detects a small set of stack files, creates <code>.gateignore</code> if absent, appends environment patterns to <code>.gitignore</code>, and attempts both pre-commit and pre-push installation. It does not create <code>.gaterc</code>.</p>

<h2 id="install"><code>gate install</code> / <code>gate uninstall</code></h2>
<p><code>gate install</code> manages the pre-commit hook only. It preserves non-Gate hook content using sentinel comments. <code>gate uninstall</code> removes that Gate pre-commit section only; it does not remove the pre-push hook installed by <code>gate init</code>. There is no CLI flag to select pre-push today.</p>

<div class="warning-box"><strong>Generated-hook environment mismatch:</strong> the hook exports <code>GATE_HOOK_TYPE=pre-commit</code>, while the CLI's verification suppression and automatic re-stage branches check <code>GATE_PRE_COMMIT</code>. As a result, <code>verify: true</code> in config can make a generated hook send credentials even though the hook command does not include <code>--verify</code>, and fixer changes may not take the intended pre-commit re-stage path. Keep verification false for hooks and inspect the index after any interactive fix.</div>

<h2 id="vault"><code>gate vault</code></h2>
<div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate vault keygen</span><span class="dim">          # use --force to replace the key</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate vault encrypt "value"</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate vault decrypt "base64-blob"</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate vault env .env</span><span class="dim">         # writes .env.encrypted</span></div>
<p>The key lives at <code>~/.gate/vault.key</code> (or under <code>GATE_HOME</code>) and is auto-created when needed. Encryption is AES-256-GCM with a random IV. The key and ciphertext live on the same machine; this is local encryption, not a managed secret store.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Putting plaintext on the command line can expose it through shell history or process inspection.</li>
  <li><code>decrypt</code> prints plaintext to stdout and expects the base64 blob, not a <code>VAULT:</code> prefix.</li>
  <li><code>vault env</code> leaves the original file in place and writes <code>&lt;file&gt;.encrypted</code>; each non-empty value becomes <code>VAULT:&lt;full-base64-blob&gt;</code>.</li>
  <li>The short <code>VAULT:&lt;id&gt;</code> created by interactive remediation has no public CLI retrieval command and no application runtime integration.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="audit"><code>gate audit</code></h2>
<div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate audit</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate audit --since 7d --filter severity=high</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate audit --stats</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate audit --verify</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate audit --export json</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate audit --export csv</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate audit --clear</span></div>
<p><code>--since</code> accepts dates or <code>Nd</code>/<code>Nw</code>/<code>Nm</code>; <code>--until</code> accepts a date. The generic filter parser accepts one simple <code>key=value</code> and the query layer recognizes decision, minimum findings, and severity fields. <code>--clear</code> asks for confirmation only in a TTY.</p>
<p>The audit log can currently retain the raw <code>secret</code> field. Export may therefore disclose credentials. Inspect the file before using export.</p>

<h2 id="report"><code>gate report</code></h2>
<div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate report</span><span class="dim">                   # Markdown file</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate report --format html</span><span class="dim">     # HTML file</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate report --format json</span><span class="dim">     # stdout, experimental adapter</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate report --incident ID</span></div>
<p>Compliance Markdown/HTML reads the current local audit log; it does not scan. See <a href="/gate/wiki/compliance/">Reports and Compliance Mappings</a>.</p>

<h2 id="history"><code>gate purge</code></h2>
<p>Scans added lines in the last 50 commits by default, or <code>--history N</code>, then writes a review-only <code>git-filter-repo</code> script and plaintext replacements outside the repository under <code>~/.gate/purge</code>. It does not execute or push the rewrite.</p>

<h2 id="status"><code>gate status</code>, <code>gate update</code>, <code>gate version</code></h2>
<ul>
  <li><code>status</code> prints local health information and initiates an update check.</li>
  <li><code>update</code> checks npm and can install a newer global package.</li>
  <li><code>version</code> prints the version and asynchronously checks for an update.</li>
  <li><code>help</code> or <code>--help</code> prints built-in help; some help claims can lag implementation.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="environment">Environment variables Gate actually reads</h2>
<table class="cmd-table">
  <thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>GATE_HOME</code></td><td>Overrides the local Gate state directory.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>GATE_OFFLINE=1</code></td><td>Skips Gate's verification and update-check requests.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>NO_COLOR</code></td><td>Disables ANSI color.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>DEBUG=1</code></td><td>Prints stack traces for top-level unexpected errors; those traces can contain paths or data.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>GATE_SKIP=1</code></td><td>Generated-hook bypass.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>GATE_ALLOW_MISSING=1</code></td><td>Lets a generated hook continue when Gate cannot be found.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>GATE_NODE_PATH</code></td><td>Explicit Node executable used by generated hooks.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>NODE_TLS_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED=0</code></td><td>Causes Gate verification to skip for safety; do not disable TLS validation globally.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><code>GATE_LOG_LEVEL</code> is not implemented. <code>GATE_HOOK_TYPE</code> is exported by generated hooks but is not consumed by the current CLI branches described above.</p>
